include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('Price Adjustments: workarounds for a routine discount | Aaron Benjamin', 'I designed Home Depot Order Up markdowns to preserve order context while keeping manager-approval rules explicit.', '/price-adjustments.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--price-adjustments/price-adjustments--cart-2x.png')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell
        +folioHeader('work', 'Price Adjustments', '/#price-adjustments')
        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    figure.folio-case__hero-media
                        img(src="/assets/img/folio/project--price-adjustments/price-adjustments--cart-2x.png" alt="Order Up cart with an Apply markdown fly-out for a damaged-item discount" width="2410" height="840" fetchpriority="high")
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow The Home Depot · Price Adjustments
                        h1 A routine discount had become a workaround.
                        p.folio-case__dek Associates had workarounds for competitor matches and damaged-item discounts. I designed a markdown path in Order Up that kept the order visible and approval rules explicit.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Staff UX Designer
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Discovery across 12 stores, markdown interactions, and usability testing
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd A fly-out preserves cart context while an associate applies a markdown
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd A 50-store pilot followed by chain-wide rollout; reported impact remains directional

                +caseSection({labelledby: "pa-stores-title"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                    h2#pa-stores-title Reason codes didn’t cover what happened on the floor
                    p On the store floor, a competitor match or a damaged item was an ordinary reason to change a price. In the software, associates had to find their own way through it. The opportunity was to bring that everyday judgment into the product without losing the controls the business needed.
                    p A competitor match or damaged-item discount was a routine request, but Order Up did not give associates a consistent way to handle it. Workarounds lengthened transactions, and the official reason codes did not cover the situations on the floor.
                    p Observation and interviews across 12 stores showed that managers trusted associates to make good calls. The product needed to support that judgment without discarding the business controls.
                    p Operations partners defined the constraints: adjustments over $50 needed manager approval, each store had a satisfaction budget, and order-level markdowns had to allocate across SKUs. Transaction time mattered to the business.

                +imageBrief({"role": "Context", "title": "A routine request across multiple screens", "composition": "Draw an associate checking an order, calculating a markdown, and seeking approval. Keep the diagram conceptual rather than reproducing an undocumented workaround.", "purpose": "Establish the opening tension.", "caption": "Conceptual context: price changes needed order visibility and business controls.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "pa-usability-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#pa-usability-title Testing favored a fly-out that kept the order visible
                        p I iterated item-level and order-level concepts, then tested task completion, transaction time, and perceived usability. Associates needed the order visible while multitasking across apps and explaining the discount math to customers. “Markdown” was more familiar than “adjustment.”
                        p The fly-out performed best on task completion and perceived usability. I developed it into apply, update, and remove paths, with the order summary showing the discount and the approval requirement remaining explicit.
                    +storyFlow({id: "pa-approval-caption", title: "Markdown workflow and constraints", nodes: [{label: "Choose scope", detail: "Apply to an item or the whole order."}, {label: "Set markdown", detail: "Enter reason, amount, and quantity."}, {label: "Check approval", detail: "Discounts over $50 require a manager."}, {label: "Review cart", detail: "Show allocation and the updated total."}], caption: "The fly-out preserves the cart while the approval rule remains explicit."})
                    +caseFigure({labelledby: "pa-apply-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--price-adjustments/price-adjustments--cart-2x.png", alt: "Apply markdown fly-out with reason, amount, and quantity beside the order summary", width: "2410", height: "840", loading: "lazy", caption: "The associate can set the markdown without losing the item and order context.", captionId: "pa-apply-caption"})
                    +caseFigure({labelledby: "pa-update-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--price-adjustments/price-adjustments--update-complete-2x.png", alt: "Update markdown fly-out and cart row showing original price and discount", width: "2390", height: "1170", loading: "lazy", caption: "Update and remove paths retain the calculation in the cart.", captionId: "pa-update-caption"})



                +imageBrief({"role": "Process", "title": "The decision and its alternatives", "composition": "Compare conceptual modal and fly-out approaches with the cart still visible; annotate the reported usability preference without numerical scores.", "purpose": "Make the research-to-decision connection visible.", "caption": "A reconstructed comparison of the choices described in this story.", "ratio": "3:2", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "pa-engagement"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                    h2#pa-engagement Keep associate judgment and business controls together
                    p Operations partners supplied the rules the interaction had to preserve: manager approval above $50, a store satisfaction budget, and allocation across SKUs for an order-level markdown. Those constraints were part of the experience, not details to append after the interface was chosen.
                    p Observation showed that managers trusted associates to make sound calls. I connected that finding to a workflow in which the associate could see the order and explain the discount while working. The product could support judgment and maintain approval requirements in the same interaction.
                +caseSection({labelledby: "pa-action"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                    h2#pa-action Keep the calculation beside the conversation
                    p I developed apply, update, and remove paths for item-level and order-level markdowns. The order summary kept the discount visible while the approval rule remained explicit. This made the familiar store task a consistent product path, with the same context available as an associate explained it to a customer.
                +caseOutcome("pa-outcome-title", "From a 50-store pilot to chain-wide rollout")
                    p The markdown feature was piloted in 50 stores, then rolled out to all Home Depot stores. The project presentation reports lower transaction times, reduced shrink, stronger standard-procedure adoption, and associates feeling more empowered at 30, 60, and 90 days after launch.
                    p.folio-case-results__source These retrospective trends are directional; no numerical magnitudes are available, and they have not been independently revalidated.
                    p My takeaway: preserving order context and using the associate’s terminology made the approval rules easier to work within. Flexibility and business controls belonged in the same workflow.
                +imageBrief({"role": "Outcome", "title": "What the evidence supports", "composition": "Use only the results stated in this case. Keep population, timeframe, qualitative status, and any attribution limits beside the result.", "purpose": "Resolve the story without overstating impact.", "caption": "Evidence summary reconstructed from the documented account; no invented measurements.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Evidence summary"})
            +folioFooter(false, 'I’m happy to walk through the prototypes, testing, and approval constraints.', {href: '/classroom-assignment-management.html', label: 'Next: Classroom Assignment Management'})
